    }), "\n", _jsxs(_components.p, {
      children: ["Query strings excel in scenarios involving resource filtering, pagination, sorting, and search operations. They align naturally with HTTP's GET method semantics, which the specification defines as safe and idempotent-suitable for retrieval operations that don't modify server state. This alignment has practical benefits: GET requests with query strings are cacheable by browsers, CDNs, and proxy servers without special configuration. They appear in browser history and server logs, making debugging straightforward. Users can bookmark filtered views or search results because the complete request state is encoded in the URL. These characteristics make query strings the obvious choice for operations like ", _jsx(_components.code, {
